WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:14.330
RUPP: Well, I'm very pleased, delighted to
welcome all of you to this Sorensen Distinguished

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:20.500
Lecture on the U.N. And Gillian Sorensen is
here. I want to express appreciation from

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.259
all of us to you, Gillian. Maybe you could
even stand up.

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:30.989
(APPLAUSE)

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:40.199
I have the privilege of introducing Valerie
Amos who will be our Gillian Sorensen lecturer.

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:47.970
Valerie was born in Georgetown, Guyana. She
went -- moved to the U.K. when she was nine

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:56.100
years old and was educated in the U.K. I just
asked her because her accent was so flawlessly

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:03.609
U.K. whether her first language was also English
-- British English -- and the answer is, it

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:04.609
was.

00:01:04.609 --> 00:01:09.860
She's had a very distinguished career for
more than twenty-five years in international

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:18.090
relations in very senior leadership positions.
Since July 2010, she was -- she has been the

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:24.440
U.N. under-general-secretary for humanitarian
affairs and emergency relief coordinator.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.720
For those of us involved in these sorts of
events, we all -- we think of her as the head

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.990
of OCHA.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:37.770
She has also served as a U.K. high commissioner
to Australia. She has served as well in the

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:43.520
U.K. Cabinet as the minister responsible for
international development.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.200
A very distinguished career; lots more to
it. You can read about it in your program.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:54.979
I now have the great honor of presenting to
you Valerie Amos who will speak for about

00:01:54.979 --> 00:02:00.479
fifteen minutes after which she and I will
have a brief conversation and then we'll open

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:07.960
it up for your questions and comments.
Thank you very much for being with us.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:19.530
(APPLAUSE)
AMOS: Professor Rupp, thank you very much,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.470
indeed. And thank you, everyone. Good evening.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.260
It's a great pleasure to be here at the Council
on Foreign Relations, an institution with

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.360
I think has provided a platform for critical
engagement and discussion into the pressing

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:40.599
issues of our time -- global governance, conflict,
peace, human rights and security.

00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:45.470
And I'm particularly pleased to be joining
a list of distinguished contributors to the

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:52.599
Sorensen Lecture Series on the United Nations.
The commitment of Ted C. Sorensen, a trusted

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:59.950
advisor to President Kennedy, and to Gillian
Martin Sorensen to the U.N. principles is

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.510
invaluable. And this lecture series, I think,
is critical because it gives the United Nations

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:12.720
and its senior members an opportunity to reflect
on our work, on our principles and the challenges

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.420
that are facing us.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:22.069
For me, this lecture comes at a particularly
critical time when the world is grappling

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:28.459
with a series of challenges which are bringing
us closer together and also pushing us wider

00:03:28.459 --> 00:03:35.689
apart, and when we are seeking to redefine
the role of the nation state, of governments

00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:42.760
and of the United Nations, given the complex
set of challenges which are facing us.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:49.910
This week alone, horrific events in France
and Nigeria have led, for example, to searching

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:57.569
questions about the nature of terrorism, security,
freedom of speech and religion, the limits

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:05.400
or otherwise of press freedom, tolerance,
racism, inequality, the impact of social media

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:11.489
and the Internet, the lack of connectivity
between people and cultures and the quality

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:14.140
of global leadership.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.410
And it's clear that we do not have all the
answers as we're dealing with phenomena which

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:27.750
are very often outside our experience -- reports
of a ten-year-old suicide bomber, a young

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:34.960
girl; deliberate targeted killing of journalists,
cartoonists whose job it was to challenge

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:42.090
us to look at our world in different ways;
and anti-Semitic attacks aimed at further

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.960
fueling tensions between Jews and Muslims.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:53.790
And in the midst of that hatred and brutality,
an outpouring of global solidarity, even as

00:04:53.790 --> 00:05:00.000
citizens question themselves and their leaders
about the kind of world we're living in and

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.230
the kind of future we want to have.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:10.670
Our world seems to be in turmoil. And nowhere
is this more evident than in the work that

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:18.800
I do in humanitarian affairs; so many contradictions
played out daily as we see the impact of conflict

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.980
and crisis on people's day-to-day lives.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:32.370
I've often said that in our work, we see the
worst of humanity and the very best -- mindless

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:39.501
violence, abuse, particularly of women and
children, and in the midst of it all, people

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:47.800
sharing what little they have, supporting,
protecting and helping each other.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.840
Humanitarian work is under significant pressure
as we face growing needs around the world.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:06:03.270
This year, 2015, 78 million people in twenty-two
countries require urgent humanitarian assistance

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:11.970
and we estimate that it will cost $16.4 billion
for us to help them. That money will provide

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:18.530
urgently needed shelter, essential health
care, education, food. It will help people

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:20.370
to survive.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:26.900
But what it will not do is help people to
rebuild their lives because without resolution

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:32.970
to conflict, people will continue to flee
brutality; and without early warning systems

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:38.120
and risk mitigation measures, people will
continue to suffer the impact of hurricanes,

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:44.920
drought, floods and other natural disasters.
Without investment in mitigation and other

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:50.000
measures, climate change will deepen the impact
of recurrent natural disasters.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.240
The United Nations was founded to promote
global peace and security, to protect people's

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:03.080
human rights and to facilitate development.
Every year, more is expected of the U.N. And

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:10.630
yet, it is an organization which is limited
not by the vision of its founders but by its

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:19.050
members today -- 193 countries, each with
different interests and perspectives reflecting

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.510
the changes we have seen in the nearly seventy
years of the organization's history.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:31.130
The United Nations has needed to change as
the world has changed and we have seen some

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:38.690
significant successes. Just in my work, the
United Nation provides food to 90 million

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:46.210
people in 80 countries every year; vaccinates
58 percent of the world's children, saving

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:55.130
3 million lives a year; assists over 38.7
million refugees and people feeling war, famine

00:07:55.130 --> 00:08:04.970
or prosecution; keeps peace with 120,000 peacekeepers
in sixteen operations on four continents;

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:12.000
fights poverty; helps to improve the health
and well-being of 420 million of the rural

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:20.720
poor and promotes and protects human rights
through some 80 treaties and declarations.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:28.910
But somehow we forget all that because we
focus particularly, I think, on the political

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:34.460
side of the United Nations -- the side of
the United Nations which uses diplomacy to

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:41.220
prevent conflict and assists some sixty countries
a year with their elections.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:48.750
But as with all organizations, with growth
has come more bureaucracy, less flexibility.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:53.610
And we have seen changes in power dynamics
between countries and regions reflected in

00:08:53.610 --> 00:09:01.350
the ways that issues are debated and discussed
-- indeed, in the way that they are resolved.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:07.960
And we see this, as well, in humanitarian
work. More is expected of us than ever before.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:13.520
We're called on to provide life-saving assistance
and protection of civilians, but we're not

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:17.130
necessarily given the tools to do it.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:22.110
Civilians are killed and injured in targeted
or indiscriminate attacks in violation of

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:30.720
international humanitarian law and often with
complete impunity. I cannot remember the number

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:37.350
of times I have reported to the Security Council
and asked them to make strong statements,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:43.530
which they do, about what is happening in
the world, but it doesn't necessarily change

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.930
what is happening on the ground.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:52.560
Eighty-two percent of the people who were
killed or injured by explosive weapons in

00:09:52.560 --> 00:10:00.430
2013, the last time we have figures, were
civilians. Violence and other forms of persecution

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:09.360
force an average of 23,000 people per day
-- per day -- to leave their homes to seek

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.130
protection elsewhere.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:18.050
Too often, humanitarian organizations are
called on to fill the glaring gaps that emerge

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:25.360
when states neglect to fulfill their duty
to safeguard their citizens. Think, for example,

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:32.120
Syria or South Sudan; or where, as a result
of conflict, the state apparatus has become

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:39.450
weak, fragmented or almost nonexistent, as
in the case of Somalia or the Central African

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.110
Republic.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:46.240
Humanitarian actors are increasingly being
called on to deal with the consequences of

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:54.220
crises that essentially have their roots in
a complex set of interrelated factors -- poor

00:10:54.220 --> 00:11:02.140
governance, political paralysis, underdevelopment,
rising levels of poverty and inequality. And

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:07.410
these dynamics in many countries are overlaid
by the growth of terrorists and radical armed

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:15.960
groups and challenges to democratization,
which create further instability. Add to this

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:22.560
mix climate change, environmental degradation,
population growth in some parts of the world

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:30.350
and the consequences of increasing levels
of internal displacement and forced migration.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:39.750
The average length of conflict-induced development
is a staggering seventeen years. Despite the

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:46.420
economic gains we have seen in many countries,
inequality is rising and poverty and instability

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.860
often go hand-in-hand.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:57.260
Half of the world's extreme poor live in fragile
states. We're seeing a convergence of global

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:06.550
trends which is increasing the risk of major
crises, their scope and complexity.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:12.440
Conflict and complex emergencies drove over
75 percent of humanitarian response needs

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:19.150
last year and most of the conflicts we are
responding to have implications far beyond

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:25.250
their borders. The crises in Iraq and Syria,
for example, have consequences across the

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.400
whole of the Middle East and beyond.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:33.440
The fall of Gaddafi in Libya led to major
insecurity and spread of weapons across Western

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:41.160
Central Africa, the impact of which is still
being felt today. And in many of the conflict

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:47.250
zones in which we work, there has been a manifest
failure by political leaders to be inclusive

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:54.650
in their protection of their people and to
realize that state sovereignty is a responsibility

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:59.949
rather than one of the spoils of being in
power.

00:12:59.949 --> 00:13:04.840
Humanitarian workers, because of the lifesaving
nature of our work, need to be impartial;

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:12.300
we need to be neutral. We have to be on the
side of the people, not on the side of governments

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.750
or armed groups where there is conflict.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:22.130
This means that we operate in highly-pressurized
and political environments where attempts

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:30.470
are made to use humanitarian action to pursue
political or security ends. This has become

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:36.630
more and more challenging as we have worked
to keep humanitarian response separate from

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:43.260
political imperatives in places like Syria,
Iraq, Ukraine and Gaza.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:50.570
We have a responsibility to be strong advocates
for the people caught in the midst of conflict.

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:59.530
And many governments don't like what we say.
And we are constantly, literally and figuratively,

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.580
under fire.

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:06.530
Working in conflict zones where international
humanitarian law is flagrantly violated is

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:17.410
dangerous. In 2013, violence against humanitarian
aid operations hit an all-time high with 251

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:26.279
separate attacks in which 155 aid workers
were killed and 134 were kidnapped.

00:14:26.279 --> 00:14:32.920
But despite the continued danger, humanitarian
groups continue to provide life-saving assistance

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:41.279
to millions of people around the world every
single day. And we do everything we can to

00:14:41.279 --> 00:14:45.020
ensure that we can continue to work.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:51.290
We negotiate with governments and armed groups;
we use those with influence over warring parties

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:56.790
to help us gain access; we use the Security
Council to push for resolutions which will

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:04.870
make a difference. We have to be operationally
effective and we have to be politically astute.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:11.350
We have to be strong advocates, good communicators.
We have to make the case, we have to raise

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:19.390
the money. And crucially, we have to be good
leaders, demonstrating impact and pushing

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.800
for change.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:27.710
The challenges facing organizations working
in the humanitarian field is a microcosm of

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:35.920
the challenges facing the entire United Nations.
How do we live up to the values of the U.N.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:43.380
charter? How do we safeguard human rights,
protect civilians and help secure a more peaceful

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:51.089
and a more just world? How do we promote more
active global citizenship? And how do we help

00:15:51.089 --> 00:15:57.610
the people of the world to connect and see
the value of inclusivity as well as of diversity

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:00.020
and difference?

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:05.060
From our work, we know that the only way to
break the cycle of violence which continues

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:10.870
to threaten global peace and stability is
for political actors to work with communities

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:18.100
to find sustainable solutions to these crises.
The need is greater than ever for states to

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:23.870
live up to their responsibility to protect
civilians from harm, and when states fail

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:31.360
to do so for others, including multilateral
institutions, to step in.

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:37.110
The tools available to the international community
now appear extremely limited in light of the

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:44.430
complex set of challenges we face. We need
a stronger and, dare I say it, perhaps more

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:51.540
interventionist global architecture to deal
with the humanitarian consequences of conflict.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:58.290
I recognize that this would come with major
risks, given global power dynamics and other

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:06.079
differences around the world. But I do have
to say that after every crisis, we say 'never

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:10.860
again', and yet, it always happens again.

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:16.770
We have a body of international humanitarian
law which over time has given us the means

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:24.100
to tackle the challenges arising from conflicts
-- a set of rules. The problem is its lack

00:17:24.100 --> 00:17:26.860
of implementation.

00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:32.170
We will, of course, continue to push for civilians
to be protected in conflict, be it calling

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:37.560
on U.N. member states to deliver on their
duty to protect their citizens or highlighting

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:44.040
to governments and militaries the devastating
impact that the use of explosive weapons has

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:48.970
on people living in densely populated urban
areas.

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:53.650
Implementation requires stronger vision and
commitment from governments and multilateral

00:17:53.650 --> 00:18:01.300
institutions, as well as from humanitarian
agencies. It is also no longer acceptable

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:07.390
that still less than half of 1 percent of
all international aid is spent on disaster

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:11.110
preparedness and prevention.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:16.770
Finding the right approach to these and other
challenges is a priority for the consultations

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:24.760
leading up to the 2016 World Humanitarian
Summit, the first ever such summit which has

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:32.300
been called for by the U.N. secretary general.
At that summit, we will have a unique opportunity

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:40.370
to reshape our approach to humanitarian aid,
and indeed, the way that we do humanitarian

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:42.800
business.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:51.960
We all face a choice. We can continue to affect
peaceful change, or we can truly transform

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:58.870
the way we approach complex problems in a
way that better reflects today's reality.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:06.430
I think we should try to do the latter, for
the sake of the millions of people around

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:14.000
the world today who frankly deserve better
from all of us. Thank you very much.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:15.360
(APPLAUSE)

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:19.440
RUPP: Thank you.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:28.960
AMOS: I hope that's not my phone.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:30.320
(LAUGHTER)

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:35.300
RUPP: I'm sure you turned it off just as everyone
else.

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:36.790
AMOS: I tried.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:44.870
RUPP: Valerie, thank you very much for that
accurate description of just how somber the

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:52.790
current situation we all face is, and also
the very upbeat portrayal of the humanitarian

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:58.429
mandate that you have lived now for these
multiple years. Thank you on both counts.

00:19:58.429 --> 00:20:06.770
I share all of your hopes and aspirations
for how we might be able to do better. But

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:11.510
I -- I guess I'd like in a -- the couple minutes
we have before we open up to questions to

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:18.549
hear a little bit more of your experience
down on the ground -- down to earth.

00:20:18.549 --> 00:20:24.920
You've had the really rare experience of being
in a leadership position for an individual

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:30.240
influential country, namely, Great Britain,
and also in -- in your role, your very senior

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:32.030
role at the U.N.

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:36.620
Maybe you could tell us a little bit about
an example of a high point and a low point

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:42.330
in -- in each of those involvements or ways
in which the two roles are different.

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:51.520
AMOS: I suppose the thing that I -- I think
is the lowest point for me, and many people

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:58.860
tell me it should not be, is that I really
feel that we have failed the people of Syria.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:05.920
I've been in this job for four years and Syria
has really, I think, defined the time that

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.720
I have been in this job.

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:14.660
And when I first visited Syria four years
ago, I was trying to persuade the government

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:20.670
that they had a humanitarian crisis on their
hands as a result of the conflict. And at

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:27.590
that time, we were talking about a million
people inside Syria in -- in need of some

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:33.280
form of humanitarian assistance. In just four
years, that number has climbed to over 12

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:34.280
million.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:39.420
I mean, we have watched that happen; we as
a world have watched that happen. More than

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:44.840
3 million people have fled to neighboring
countries. The -- the brutality, the level

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:48.530
of brutality that people are facing is indescribable.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:56.710
I have personally, and so have my colleagues,
spent a huge amount of time getting aid. I

00:21:56.710 --> 00:22:05.250
mean, I think it's -- it -- what -- what humanitarian
workers have been able to continue doing in

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:11.950
Syria is absolutely extraordinary in the most
difficult and dangerous circumstances. And

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:17.470
we are feeding millions of people, getting
health care to millions of people, but I still

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:28.929
feel that we have failed because we have watched
-- we have watched a country descend into

00:22:28.929 --> 00:22:34.530
-- into war and conflict and we haven't been
able to stop it.

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:43.760
And I think I, with everyone in a position
of some responsibility and authority, needs

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:49.830
to take some responsibility for that, whatever
I have been able to do. And people keep reminding

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:58.470
me that I have been able to do a lot. So,
I see that as -- I see that very much as a

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:00.070
low point.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:06.970
I think as a high point, I -- I -- in my career
and in the work that I do, it is just the

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:14.440
extraordinary people that I meet every single
day. I mean, I've been to some of the worst

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:25.780
places in the world -- Central African Republic,
Somalia, Afghanistan, South Sudan, Democratic

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:32.600
Republic of the Congo. I've watched people
fleeing violence; I've watched children on

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:41.140
the brink of death in malnutrition centers.
And yet, I've seen the extraordinary hope

00:23:41.140 --> 00:23:47.860
that people have that the world will help
and support them and that they will come out

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:49.180
of this.

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:53.880
And I've seen people want to help themselves
from day one. I remember last year when I

00:23:53.880 --> 00:24:02.880
went to the Philippines within days of Typhoon
Haiyan and Tacloban was absolutely devastated.

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:06.540
There was hardly a building left standing.

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:14.850
And yet -- yet, people within hours were trying
to find ways of creating, you know, shelter

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:21.140
for their families whilst at the same time
trying to find the bodies of those that were

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:27.370
missing. I mean, and I kept saying, "This
is a resilience that -- that we should -- a

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:33.470
level of resilience that we should not expect
people to have, and yet they do have it."

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:42.000
So, you know, this has been the most extraordinary
job I've ever had, precisely because in the

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:50.280
midst of utter devastation, people remain
strong and extremely positive.

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:56.970
RUPP: Well, that's -- having shared with you
going to all of those places you mentioned,

00:24:56.970 --> 00:25:03.390
I certainly agree with that. And I think it
is a -- when people ask you, well, doesn't

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:08.650
it get depressing, the response to that is
the one you just gave -- is as awful as it

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:14.120
is, there are people who are -- demonstrate
the resilience and the -- and the will to

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:18.820
not only help themselves, but help others
-- that it brings out the best in people.

00:25:18.820 --> 00:25:27.240
But let's go back to the low point, to Syria.
As you know, the International Rescue Committee

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:32.240
is involved in Syria and in all the countries
surrounding Syria on all of the fronts that

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:33.240
you mentioned.

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:42.160
And I -- it has -- the crisis has accelerated
to a point where it is absolutely an incredible

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:49.150
tragedy for all those involved. And we in
the international community have watched it

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:55.290
accelerate from a very small problem to now
into the largest problem that the international

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:58.150
community has faced in a long time.

00:25:58.150 --> 00:26:04.630
You mentioned that -- that international relief
and development organizations have to exemplify

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:12.590
impartiality in the way in which their goods
or services are distributed. You mentioned

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:18.940
neutrality. You didn't mention independence,
but I'm sure you agree operational independence

00:26:18.940 --> 00:26:21.620
is also critical.

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:27.440
Given the commitment to neutrality, in particular,
how -- how do you think we can or should have

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:31.400
been more effective in dealing -- we in the
humanitarian community -- with the crisis

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.580
in Syria?

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:38.820
AMOS: I think it -- I think it's not just
a problem for the humanitarian community.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:43.350
I mean, I think that -- I think that is part
of the problem. The big issue in Syria is

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:54.340
that we have not managed to find a political
solution to a major political crisis in -- in

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:55.340
that country.

00:26:55.340 --> 00:27:00.790
So, you know, humanitarian organizations and
humanitarian workers have been doing everything

00:27:00.790 --> 00:27:06.340
they can -- so, working from inside Syria,
working across borders from Turkey, Jordan.

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:14.230
I mean, there are some NGOs doing extraordinary
work that have been working, particularly

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:20.630
from Turkey, over many, many years, establishing
relations with communities in the north of

00:27:20.630 --> 00:27:28.250
Syria that has actually allowed them to continue
working there even now when those areas are

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:35.260
under ISIL control. We don't know how much
longer that will last but they're able to

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:41.780
work there because the communities themselves
want them to be there and are challenging

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:48.120
ISIL as ISIL is trying to get those organizations
out.

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:53.200
I think that there are -- you know, there
were questions raised, for example, about

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:58.980
the fact that the United Nations was working
inside Syria, working with the government

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:07.309
of Syria to get aid across conflict lines
inside Syria. I absolutely defend the decision

00:28:07.309 --> 00:28:15.960
to do that because our job was to try to reach
all Syrians in need of aid, and this is a

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:24.540
government that still controls territory and
still -- still -- still controls a security

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:25.910
apparatus, for example.

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:30.370
We work with the government; we've worked
with -- with armed groups. It's been a very

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:36.510
challenging environment because of the proliferation
of different armed groups on the ground.

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:42.320
Perhaps we could have pressured the Security
Council earlier to get some of the resolutions

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:50.420
that we did. But the -- the political differences,
particularly between the permanent five of

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:57.990
the Security Council, I think really led to
a degree of stasis at the beginning of the

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:03.799
crisis because there simply wasn't an agreed
way of moving forward, even though I think

00:29:03.799 --> 00:29:10.430
on humanitarian issues, there was a sense
in which everyone agreed that more needed

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:14.160
to be done to deal with the humanitarian consequences
of conflict.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:21.200
RUPP: In your comments just now you mentioned
about the -- the growth of programs in -- in

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:26.929
the United Nations and -- and therefore an
increase in bureaucracy and a reduction in

00:29:26.929 --> 00:29:36.200
flexibility. That last comment about the difficulty
in the response of the U.N. would call further

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:40.980
attention to that set of issues. So, what's
the long-term solution to that problem?

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:45.270
AMOS: One of the things that we're looking
at in the run-up to this World Humanitarian

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:51.929
Summit is essentially how do we do our business.
Are we doing -- are we conducting humanitarian

00:29:51.929 --> 00:29:59.400
operations in the best possible way? Should
we be working with a wider range of partners?

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:06.040
What is the -- what is the added value of
U.N. organizations in these situations? How

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:14.400
can you best support the local communities
on the ground who are there and present and

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:15.400
already have the relationship?

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:22.840
So, there are a big set of questions, I think,
which we have to continue to ask ourselves

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:32.660
about what we're doing, why we're doing it,
what needs the support, if you like, of the

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:38.850
multilateral system, as opposed to what can
be done at the local level. And it's -- it's

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:44.150
-- it's extraordinarily difficult in conflict
situations. It's much more straightforward

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:45.640
in a natural disaster situation.

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:53.710
But in a conflict situation where you have
so many different individuals and organizations

00:30:53.710 --> 00:31:00.850
on the ground at war with each other, I think
you need the United Nations and, as it were,

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:06.669
the -- the independence of the United Nations
system. But therein also lies some challenges,

00:31:06.669 --> 00:31:11.360
because of course the United Nations has,
you know, it has a political dimension, it

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:15.130
has a peacekeeping dimension, a development
dimension, a humanitarian dimension; we are

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:18.650
not always seen as neutral. We're not always
seen as independent.

00:31:18.650 --> 00:31:23.700
RUPP: Well, let's just -- and then -- then
we will open up to others. But let's take

00:31:23.700 --> 00:31:33.010
one last example. We've all been struck with
the Ebola crisis in West Africa. That's an

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:39.990
example where the question of lack of flexibility
or rigidity or organizational interaction

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:46.110
can't help but arise.Could you comment on
how -- give a grade to the international community

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:50.470
on responding to Ebola, or international community
and West African communities.

00:31:50.470 --> 00:31:57.840
AMOS: I think Ebola is -- is probably the
most complex coordination challenge that the

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:04.270
international community has ever had to -- to
face. Because this is not just a health crisis;

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:14.730
this is a major economic crisis for the countries
of West Africa. It's a major social crisis

00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:23.630
for those countries, as well, with some big
questions and challenges around how communities

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:26.160
traditionally operate.

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:33.630
We make lots of assumptions, I think, in the
criticisms that have been made of the Ebola

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:40.559
response about how we think health care is
managed in those countries. These are countries

00:32:40.559 --> 00:32:48.470
that are post-conflict countries -- very poor
infrastructure. In most of those countries,

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:56.440
in the rural areas, people probably haven't
even seen a clinic or a health worker.

00:32:56.440 --> 00:33:05.370
So, to then suddenly try to put in place structures
to help to deal with something as contagious

00:33:05.370 --> 00:33:10.880
as Ebola, particularly when you're saying
to people that your traditional practices

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:17.070
in terms of how you bury your dead can no
longer be carried out, I mean, it is a -- it's

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:22.040
a -- it's a huge challenge, not just for the
U.N., but for the whole international community,

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:24.770
and particularly for the governments of those
countries.

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:32.540
I think we all accept that we were too slow
in terms of bringing those coordination elements

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:40.610
together. But the way that the health aspects
of this kind of replicated themselves so quickly

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:45.760
over time was something which I think surprised
us all.

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:51.580
RUPP: OK, well, thank you very much for your
-- your candor and your recognition that there

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:54.210
are challenges yet to be addressed.

00:33:54.210 --> 00:34:01.690
I would like to open it now to questions from
the floor. When I recognize you, please stand.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:10.250
Say your name and any other identification
you'd like to give and then put a question

00:34:10.250 --> 00:34:15.330
-- not -- not a speech, but a question would
be good.

00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:16.450
Yes?

00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:18.519
QUESTION: Jeff Laurenti.

00:34:18.519 --> 00:34:24.179
Thank you, Madam Under-Secretary-General,
and then for your lecture, but even more importantly,

00:34:24.179 --> 00:34:29.049
for having been doing God's work, often at
the gates of hell.

00:34:29.049 --> 00:34:36.649
I was struck by a passing reference you made,
quote, "We need stronger and, dare I say,

00:34:36.649 --> 00:34:44.739
perhaps more interventionist global architecture"
in order to deal with the emergencies, humanitarian

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:50.349
crises occasioned by conflict. And so, you
have put something very big out there.

00:34:50.349 --> 00:34:51.349
I wonder if you...

00:34:51.349 --> 00:34:52.349
AMOS: In a very gentle voice.

00:34:52.349 --> 00:34:53.349
(LAUGHTER)

00:34:53.349 --> 00:34:54.729
QUESTION: In a very gentle voice.

00:34:54.729 --> 00:35:00.640
I -- I wonder if you could suggest to us what
you personally think that architecture -- what

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:07.109
the pillars and walls or whatever of that
interventionist architecture might be. How,

00:35:07.109 --> 00:35:12.810
in situations where you have had political
kind of international unity -- Congo, Afghanistan

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:19.380
-- would it have made a difference? And how,
in situations where you haven't had unity

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:26.950
among the Security Council members -- Syria
or arguably Gaza -- would it make a difference

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:29.380
to have a so-called interventionist architecture?

00:35:29.380 --> 00:35:36.400
RUPP: Jeff, I thank you for putting a question
mark by the end of that.

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:37.549
(LAUGHTER)

00:35:37.549 --> 00:35:43.499
AMOS: It's a huge question and I wanted to
put it out there.And I don't have -- I don't

00:35:43.499 --> 00:35:47.809
have all the elements as to what the architecture
might look like.

00:35:47.809 --> 00:35:52.640
You know, a few years ago, we talked about
the concept of responsibility to protect and

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:59.319
what that might mean. And we haven't been
able to deliver on that.

00:35:59.319 --> 00:36:03.160
And it's -- it's partly because we -- you
know, what I've seen in the time that I've

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:08.070
been at the U.N. is -- and my colleagues at
the International Committee of the Red Cross

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:15.619
talk about this, too -- a more kind of aggressive
use of state sovereignty by member states

00:36:15.619 --> 00:36:23.130
of the United Nations, basically to prevent
things happening and to prevent the United

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:27.190
Nations and the sort of multilateral system
and others calling them to account.

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:33.339
And quite frankly, I -- I think that there
is not enough calling to account; that you

00:36:33.339 --> 00:36:42.219
have states that sign up to a whole set of
treaties, that sign up to the U.N. charter

00:36:42.219 --> 00:36:50.680
-- it carries responsibility with it. And
there isn't -- there isn't sufficiently -- sufficient,

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:58.890
in my view, accountability linked to that
membership of the United Nations and that,

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:07.339
as it were, almost passive acceptance of a
commitment to the values of the U.N. charter.

00:37:07.339 --> 00:37:11.789
So, you know, I don't know what the different
elements of this might look -- but, I mean,

00:37:11.789 --> 00:37:15.730
I actually feel very strongly that we have
a strong body of international humanitarian

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:25.329
law which we are not putting into practice.
I have on many occasions exhorted the Security

00:37:25.329 --> 00:37:34.089
Council members to recognize flagrant violations
of international humanitarian law -- and they

00:37:34.089 --> 00:37:37.259
do, and then there is no action after that.

00:37:37.259 --> 00:37:43.529
So, I ask -- so, I'm asking the question,
in the modern world where we are proud of

00:37:43.529 --> 00:37:48.759
that international architecture, where we
are proud of the Geneva Conventions, where

00:37:48.759 --> 00:37:53.271
we are proud of the progress that we have
made -- and we have made progress; I mean,

00:37:53.271 --> 00:37:59.760
I think part of the problem is that we focus
on the negative not on the positive, and we

00:37:59.760 --> 00:38:01.130
have made progress.

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:12.710
But at the same time, to -- to watch as, you
know, millions of people are forced to flee

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:21.559
as there is abuse on an unprecedented scale
of girls and women in many countries across

00:38:21.559 --> 00:38:27.461
the world and action is not being taken in
relation to this, I ask the question, should

00:38:27.461 --> 00:38:28.910
we not be more interventionist?

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:35.640
Now, when -- and I say there -- I say it because
when you talk about intervention, people always

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:41.609
think it means boots on the ground; it means
that you're invading somebody else's country.

00:38:41.609 --> 00:38:47.869
And that is not necessarily the question that
I'm asking. I'm asking a question about an

00:38:47.869 --> 00:38:55.829
architecture that we already have, a body
of rules and law that we already have that

00:38:55.829 --> 00:39:06.269
we are not holding ourselves accountable to.
And I see this as a significant failure.

00:39:06.269 --> 00:39:10.849
Within the United Nations ourselves, we have
been doing some soul searching on the issue

00:39:10.849 --> 00:39:17.359
of human rights and particularly post what
happened in Sri Lanka and criticisms that

00:39:17.359 --> 00:39:30.910
the United Nations did not, sufficiently early,
raise the temperature and call the fact that

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:35.420
human right -- major human rights abuses were
being carried out in Sri Lanka. And it is

00:39:35.420 --> 00:39:39.750
-- it's caused us as United Nations to completely
rethink how we do this.

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:44.609
And the secretary general and the deputy secretary-general
is now leading something called the Human

00:39:44.609 --> 00:39:52.980
Rights Up Front agenda, which is about putting
the recognition and -- and commitment to human

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:59.109
rights, which is a founding element of the
U.N. charter, front and center of our work.

00:39:59.109 --> 00:40:04.190
But it's not going to be enough for us to
do that from an organizational perspective

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:07.440
if our member states don't buy into this.

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:14.719
So, these are the questions that are -- that
I'm -- that I'm asking. And yes, we need to

00:40:14.719 --> 00:40:20.020
market it if we're associated with it but
let's not have a whole 'nother big singing,

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:23.759
dancing organization. Let's have some accountability,
I say.

00:40:23.759 --> 00:40:28.359
RUPP: OK, accountability -- that would be
very helpful. Yes?

00:40:28.359 --> 00:40:29.359
(LAUGHTER)

00:40:29.359 --> 00:40:32.690
AMOS: That's classic British understatement
from an American.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:33.690
(LAUGHTER)

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:37.819
QUESTION: Thank you, George. My name is David
Phillips with Columbia University.

00:40:37.819 --> 00:40:44.269
A couple of your predecessors -- Jan Egeland,
Sergio de Mello come to mind -- reinvented

00:40:44.269 --> 00:40:50.140
the role of emergency relief coordinator as
the U.N.'s chief advocate. In light of your

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:56.829
experience in Syria, do you feel that enough
was done to name and shame countries on the

00:40:56.829 --> 00:41:04.009
Security Council who became accomplices to
the human suffering? And is neutrality really

00:41:04.009 --> 00:41:09.980
a cherished principle, or does it merely contribute
to the misery of victims?

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:17.619
AMOS: Well, whose neutrality? Can you say
whose neutrality you're referring to? Because

00:41:17.619 --> 00:41:23.359
the neutrality that I'm talking about is the
importance for humanitarian work of being

00:41:23.359 --> 00:41:26.390
able to engage with and speak with everybody.

00:41:26.390 --> 00:41:30.869
QUESTION: So, I'm not thinking about the neutrality
of humanitarian workers. I'm really thinking

00:41:30.869 --> 00:41:36.569
specifically about your neutrality and your
voice and what you can do to mobilize the

00:41:36.569 --> 00:41:40.709
political will to take on those countries
that become accomplices to crime.

00:41:40.709 --> 00:41:49.729
AMOS: First of all, I think that -- that -- that
part of my -- my advocacy, and in a way, I

00:41:49.729 --> 00:41:55.729
think that as this rather grand title I have
as emergency relief coordinator, that part

00:41:55.729 --> 00:42:06.660
of that is actually about being on the side
of the people who are facing these atrocities.

00:42:06.660 --> 00:42:11.729
And I've been very strong about that. I think
that it is about taking sides but I think

00:42:11.729 --> 00:42:15.190
it's about taking the side of the people.

00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:20.730
On the issue of -- of calling out and naming
and shaming, this -- this gets raised with

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:28.289
me frequently, particularly in relation to
members of the Security Council. And one of

00:42:28.289 --> 00:42:35.310
the challenges I think that we have is with
respect to the issue of humanitarian work,

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:40.089
you very often find that the members of the
Security Council are speaking with one voice

00:42:40.089 --> 00:42:46.589
-- I mean, you know, they have very, very
similar views. What they disagree about is

00:42:46.589 --> 00:42:52.690
the political -- what is the political root
that will actually have an impact on the work

00:42:52.690 --> 00:42:55.269
that I'm doing.

00:42:55.269 --> 00:43:03.709
So, I rather famously once had someone say
to me, "You should stand up every week in

00:43:03.709 --> 00:43:14.329
the Security Council and denounce Russia because
of Russia's closeness to Syria." I had to

00:43:14.329 --> 00:43:21.549
actually disabuse them of the fact that that
in relation to humanitarian issues, Russia

00:43:21.549 --> 00:43:33.460
had been extremely helpful to us in terms
of the relationship that we were able to open

00:43:33.460 --> 00:43:38.160
up with the Syrian authorities to get access
to -- to various places.

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:44.609
So, it is always more complicated than it
appears on the outside because we're talking

00:43:44.609 --> 00:43:52.309
about -- we're talking political differences
which have a significant impact on the humanitarian

00:43:52.309 --> 00:43:58.410
work. But in the work that I am doing and
in the advocacy that I am doing, very often

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:06.519
I am being helped by countries which, on the
political side, have significant differences.

00:44:06.519 --> 00:44:12.950
So, that's where the challenge comes, in terms
of that advocacy.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:17.140
RUPP: Yes -- yes?

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:27.859
QUESTION: Hello, my name is -- I'm a journalist
at the -- Evelyn Leopold -- a journalist at

00:44:27.859 --> 00:44:31.440
the U.N. who sits at Baroness Amos' feet.

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:34.599
AMOS: I hadn't noticed that before, but...

00:44:34.599 --> 00:44:35.599
(LAUGHTER)

00:44:35.599 --> 00:44:43.079
QUESTION: So, my question is how careful are
you, since walls have ears, how careful are

00:44:43.079 --> 00:44:49.281
you when you brief the Security Council so
that Syria doesn't take it out on WFP or someone

00:44:49.281 --> 00:44:57.289
else who needs the government to deliver a
-- and secondly, you said something about

00:44:57.289 --> 00:45:03.180
putting humanitarian issues first. I thought
that's what the U.N. promised to do after

00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:05.119
Rwanda.

00:45:05.119 --> 00:45:11.880
AMOS: Just on your -- just on your second
point, I mean, I think that after Rwanda,

00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:21.349
after Srebrenica, after a whole variety of
crises that we've seen in -- in the world,

00:45:21.349 --> 00:45:27.880
that member states of the United Nations have
said "never again", and yet, we have not managed

00:45:27.880 --> 00:45:30.769
to live up to that.

00:45:30.769 --> 00:45:36.159
It was out of that that we got the whole issue
of, you know, the responsibility to protect

00:45:36.159 --> 00:45:43.339
-- that where you had an issue in a country
where civilians needed protecting, that somehow

00:45:43.339 --> 00:45:51.170
we would take a -- we would look at this differently.
But we have not been able to deliver on that

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:59.920
because of the importance within the U.N.
of the issue of state sovereignty and, you

00:45:59.920 --> 00:46:06.619
know, as it were, noninterference in the domestic
affairs of another country. And how you deal

00:46:06.619 --> 00:46:15.749
with that in a situation where civilians are
behind attacked, brutalized and violated is

00:46:15.749 --> 00:46:17.450
difficult.

00:46:17.450 --> 00:46:24.670
How careful I -- am I when I brief the Security
Council? I think I'm extremely frank and upfront

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:29.209
when I brief the Security Council, in what
are called close consultations, because I

00:46:29.209 --> 00:46:35.180
think it's important that members of the Security
Council who are reporting back -- ambassadors

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:41.970
who are reporting back to their capitals hear
from me what the reality is on the ground

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:47.819
and what the implications of the actions that
Security Council members and others take,

00:46:47.819 --> 00:46:52.950
have on our work.

00:46:52.950 --> 00:46:58.819
And I don't feel that I hold back. And I think
if you speak to Security Council members,

00:46:58.819 --> 00:47:02.739
they'll -- they will probably agree with that.

00:47:02.739 --> 00:47:08.869
RUPP: OK. Yes, Don?

00:47:08.869 --> 00:47:15.920
QUESTION: Donald Shriver of Union Theological
Seminary.

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:21.709
As we all know, when the U.N. started taking
seriously the principle of responsibility

00:47:21.709 --> 00:47:31.229
to protect, it was offering a real collision
with the principle of natural sovereignty.

00:47:31.229 --> 00:47:37.599
What is your reading of the experience of
the U.N. so far in undertaking responsibility

00:47:37.599 --> 00:47:45.739
to protect without merely engaging in further
violent conflict with nationally sovereign

00:47:45.739 --> 00:47:46.739
governments?

00:47:46.739 --> 00:47:55.440
AMOS: I think it's been -- I think it's been
extremely difficult to -- for the U.N. to

00:47:55.440 --> 00:48:06.660
-- to really apply the principle of responsibility
to protect because what you do have is governments

00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:16.259
which then claim sovereignty. And I think
that even after the whole development of the

00:48:16.259 --> 00:48:22.270
principle of responsibility to -- to -- to
protect, that actually nation states have

00:48:22.270 --> 00:48:31.699
become even more aggressive in their use of
state sovereignty as a reason to prevent action

00:48:31.699 --> 00:48:34.460
being taken to protect civilians.

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:43.630
We saw this in Syria where we had -- we had
to go to Security Council and get two resolutions

00:48:43.630 --> 00:48:52.680
to enable us to cross the borders from, for
example, Turkey into Syria and also Jordan

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:59.150
and Iraq into Syria across border crossing
points, which on the Syrian side were not

00:48:59.150 --> 00:49:04.789
held by the Syrian government. And I don't
think that the Syrian government has ever

00:49:04.789 --> 00:49:12.130
forgiven me; they see me as personally responsible
for pushing the Security Council to agree

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:13.749
those resolutions.

00:49:13.749 --> 00:49:22.529
For me, taking an approach which is how best
do we reach people across Syria who are in

00:49:22.529 --> 00:49:28.900
need of help and assistance -- that had to
be the cornerstone of our policy. And Security

00:49:28.900 --> 00:49:36.099
Council members agreed with that. And that's
why we got those resolutions passed.

00:49:36.099 --> 00:49:41.249
And I think that there is a very odd thing
happening, which is that on the one hand,

00:49:41.249 --> 00:49:46.859
you have governments at the United Nations
that are pushing harder and harder the issue

00:49:46.859 --> 00:49:53.359
of state sovereignty whilst at the same time,
their citizens are connecting through social

00:49:53.359 --> 00:50:00.569
media and the Internet and actually taking
less and less notice of their governments.

00:50:00.569 --> 00:50:09.510
So, on the one hand, you need governments
to -- to put in place social programs, to

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:16.540
-- to provide basic social services and so
on, but very often you have their citizens

00:50:16.540 --> 00:50:21.940
that are feeling that their governments are
becoming increasingly irrelevant. And how

00:50:21.940 --> 00:50:27.400
you deal with that contradiction I think is
also going to be a question for us for the

00:50:27.400 --> 00:50:28.400
future.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:32.880
RUPP: Well, as we turn to another question,
I can't help observing that as -- as much

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:38.380
as you had words of praise for the international
humanitarian architecture, there do seem to

00:50:38.380 --> 00:50:43.009
be some real problems that still need to be
addressed. And maybe in your next career,

00:50:43.009 --> 00:50:45.749
you could work that out.

00:50:45.749 --> 00:50:47.489
(LAUGHTER)

00:50:47.489 --> 00:50:52.979
Are there -- I see a lot of men hands. Are
there some women? Yes?

00:50:52.979 --> 00:50:59.119
QUESTION: Hi, I'm Marcia Biggs. I'm a filmmaker.
I'm working on a story on the Yazidi community

00:50:59.119 --> 00:51:02.910
in Northern Iraq, targeted and attacked by
ISIS.

00:51:02.910 --> 00:51:07.630
I'm just wondering if you consider that an
act of genocide and what the U.N. is doing

00:51:07.630 --> 00:51:12.200
to try to retrieve the estimated 3,000 girls
that are still missing and widely believed

00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:15.900
to be used as slaves and sold throughout the
region?

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:29.739
AMOS: There -- there are a number of minority
communities that have been targeted that need

00:51:29.739 --> 00:51:36.819
support. There are -- there's contradictory
information about numbers and who and where.

00:51:36.819 --> 00:51:41.680
And part of the responsibility, of course,
we have as United Nations is to try and get

00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:48.999
as much data and information as we can so
that we are operating on the basis of information

00:51:48.999 --> 00:51:52.999
that is as fact-based as possible.

00:51:52.999 --> 00:52:01.630
In terms of the reported 3,000 girls that
are reported missing, we don't have data that

00:52:01.630 --> 00:52:08.259
actually confirms that. I mean, there is a
lot of data that shows that a lot of women

00:52:08.259 --> 00:52:14.759
and children, not just from minority communities,
but -- from across Iraq, but also in Syria,

00:52:14.759 --> 00:52:22.559
have been targeted. There are widely different
-- different reports about the numbers.

00:52:22.559 --> 00:52:29.089
And what we can do from our work on the humanitarian
side is to as much as possible get information

00:52:29.089 --> 00:52:35.049
and data from different communities and the
different organizations that we are working

00:52:35.049 --> 00:52:40.450
with, from the Red Crescent societies that
we are working with, with the Gulf charitable

00:52:40.450 --> 00:52:45.759
organizations and others that are there on
the ground as to what they see and know so

00:52:45.759 --> 00:52:52.160
that as much as possible we can work to get
those young women back.

00:52:52.160 --> 00:52:54.190
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE)

00:52:54.190 --> 00:53:02.930
AMOS: It's -- I cannot say whether those numbers
are exaggerated. What I can say is that there

00:53:02.930 --> 00:53:10.209
are wildly different -- differing numbers
about how many have disappeared.

00:53:10.209 --> 00:53:18.559
In a way, you know, my perspective is one
young woman disappearing is too many. So,

00:53:18.559 --> 00:53:25.339
you know, working to ensure that this doesn't
happen, working to ensure that there is adequate

00:53:25.339 --> 00:53:31.699
protection for minorities, adequate protection
for women and girls is the crucial element

00:53:31.699 --> 00:53:33.509
in all of this.

00:53:33.509 --> 00:53:45.029
RUPP: OK, one final question. Yes? Yes? Someone
needs to give you a...

00:53:45.029 --> 00:53:51.469
QUESTION: Hi, my name is Alexandra Zevallos-Ortiz
and I'm from Austria. I'm currently interning

00:53:51.469 --> 00:53:56.319
with IPS at the United Nations and I've got
a question concerning the Syrian refugee crisis.

00:53:56.319 --> 00:54:03.319
We spoke -- or we are currently speaking about
a failure of Europe and United States, basically

00:54:03.319 --> 00:54:11.119
the Western world. And most part -- the highest
number of Syrian refugees have fled to the

00:54:11.119 --> 00:54:12.270
-- to the neighboring countries.

00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:19.819
I just wanted to know what's your position
concerning the Gulf states. Why hasn't -- haven't

00:54:19.819 --> 00:54:24.719
they established a single refugee camp within
their own borders?

00:54:24.719 --> 00:54:35.219
AMOS: First of all, I think it's important
to recognize that in all of the neighboring

00:54:35.219 --> 00:54:41.289
countries -- in Jordan, in Lebanon, in Turkey
-- the majority of Syrians are not living

00:54:41.289 --> 00:54:48.130
in camp situations. So, although there is
a kind of focus on camps -- I mean, particularly

00:54:48.130 --> 00:54:55.819
in Jordan, and a focus on Zaatari Camp -- 75
to 80 percent of Syrian refugees in Jordan

00:54:55.819 --> 00:55:02.279
are not in a camp situation. The numbers are
even higher in Lebanon. And again, the majority

00:55:02.279 --> 00:55:07.680
of refugees even in Turkey, which has set
up a number of camps on the border, are not

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:09.450
in a camp situation.

00:55:09.450 --> 00:55:19.319
So, the issue is not 'should camps be established'.
The issue is are -- is provision being made

00:55:19.319 --> 00:55:26.749
to ensure that Syrians that are fleeing violence
in Syria are able to go to other countries

00:55:26.749 --> 00:55:31.420
to be welcomed in those countries and to be
safe and secure in those countries.

00:55:31.420 --> 00:55:36.099
And what my colleague Antonio Guterres from
the High Commission of Refugees is seeking

00:55:36.099 --> 00:55:43.030
to do is to make sure that the huge burden
which is being place on particularly Jordan,

00:55:43.030 --> 00:55:51.660
Lebanon and Turkey where the majority of the
over 3 million have gone -- in Lebanon, for

00:55:51.660 --> 00:55:58.329
example, over 20 percent of the Lebanese population
is now made up of Syrian refugees. I always

00:55:58.329 --> 00:56:02.849
use my own country, the United Kingdom, as
an example -- that would be the equivalent

00:56:02.849 --> 00:56:12.779
of 12 million people turning up seeking entry
into the United Kingdom.

00:56:12.779 --> 00:56:20.459
So, one, we have to recognize the support
that those neighboring countries require.

00:56:20.459 --> 00:56:25.719
We also have to recognize that the majority
of refugees don't actually want to go too

00:56:25.719 --> 00:56:27.160
far because they want to go home.

00:56:27.160 --> 00:56:33.229
You know, they think -- you know, many -- many
people that I spoke to in the first few days

00:56:33.229 --> 00:56:37.140
-- first few months of the conflict thought
it wouldn't last very long and they would

00:56:37.140 --> 00:56:43.469
be back home by now. People are desperate
to go home to rebuild their lives. That does

00:56:43.469 --> 00:56:50.089
not take away, I think, from the responsibility
of countries across the world to recognize

00:56:50.089 --> 00:56:53.599
the major crisis that we are facing -- not
just in Syria.

00:56:53.599 --> 00:57:01.920
For the first time ever, the total number
of displaced people and refugees across the

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:10.019
world has topped 50 million people -- for
the first time since about 1945; I shouldn't

00:57:10.019 --> 00:57:17.759
say for the first time ever. These are huge
and significant numbers.

00:57:17.759 --> 00:57:22.229
And I think in each and every country has
a responsibility, particularly given that

00:57:22.229 --> 00:57:26.809
the burden's usually on the neighboring countries
and usually those neighboring countries are

00:57:26.809 --> 00:57:32.059
themselves fragile. We all have a responsibility
to look to ourselves to give what support

00:57:32.059 --> 00:57:33.469
we can.

00:57:33.469 --> 00:57:38.839
RUPP: Well, please join me in thanking Valerie
Amos for her time.

00:57:38.839 --> 00:57:39.839
(APPLAUSE)

00:57:39.839 --> 00:57:41.829
AMOS: Thank you.

00:57:41.829 --> 00:57:45.700
RUPP: Thank you all very much for coming.

00:57:45.700 --> 00:57:46.700
AMOS: Thank you.

00:57:46.700 --> 00:57:46.701
END

